ady stance, anticipating the ball.  \n- In **Frame 13**, he is lunging forward aggressively, reaching low to his left with his racket, clearly attempting to make contact with a low ball near the net.  \n- There is **no visual evidence** of:  \n  - A shoelace coming undone.  \n  - A visible slip (no skid marks, no sudden foot slide).  \n  - Ball contact causing the fall — the ball is not visible near his body at impact, and he appears to miss or barely graze it.  \n- His body is fully extended in an athletic, over-committed motion — classic “trying too hard to reach a shot.”\n\n---\n\n**6) How does Tiafoe react?**  \n✅ In **Frame 15**, he is on his hands and knees, head down — appears frustrated or exhausted, not injured.  \n✅ In **Frame 18**, he is standing, smiling, and laughing with Roger Federer — indicating no serious injury and a good-spirited reaction to his fall.  \n✅ In **Frame 19**, he is walking toward the net, still smiling — clearly not in pain, but amused by the situation.\n\n---\n\n**Sequence Trace:**\n\n- **Preparation (Frames 1–12):** Tiafoe is in position, anticipating the shot. He is focused and ready.\n- **Fall Moment (Frames 13–14):** He lunges dramatically to his left, extending fully to reach a low ball. His momentum carries him off-balance, and he falls backward onto the court.\n- **Aftermath (Frames 15–20):** He recovers quickly, shows no signs of injury, and shares a laugh with Federer — confirming the fall was due to over-exertion, not accident or injury.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\n✅ **Answer \'2\' — “He tries hard to hit the ball” — is CORRECT.**  \nThe visual evidence clearly shows Tiafoe falling as a direct result of an aggressive, full-body lunge to reach a difficult shot. His body position, the context of the play, and his subsequent reaction all support that the fall was caused by *excessive effort to hit the ball*, not by slipping, shoelace issues, or ball contact.\n\n**Note:** The set score of 0-1 and game score of 1-0 are **not v